Overlay patch 0058: FOLD IN UPSTREAM SOH `G_SETTILESIZE_LERP` (the game half).
Upstream commit `c4e92a70f` on HarbourMasters/Shipwright `develop` ("Constant-size
texture scroll display lists via G_SETTILESIZE_LERP", #6902). Pairs with overlay
0057, the LUS half.
Upstream code, no `__IOS__` gating; the macOS build builds and runs it too.

Upstream's `libultraship` submodule pointer bump is EXCLUDED — our LUS is our own
pin plus this overlay, and 0057 is how it gets the new opcode.

WHY (the same bug as 0056). `G_SETTILESIZE_INTERP` bakes ONE tile-size command
per interpolated frame into the display list, each carrying a pre-stepped
FRACTIONAL tile coordinate (`xStep / interpFrames` accumulated in a float). Those
are exactly the coordinates that land on 31.9999 and make HD replacement textures
rescale by 32/31 (see 0056's header). It also makes display list memory scale
with the interpolation frame count — a 120 Hz iPhone allocates 6x what a 20 Hz
run does for every scrolling surface. `G_SETTILESIZE_LERP` sends the two exact
ENDPOINTS once and lerps at draw time instead.

WHAT UPSTREAM CHANGES, taken as-is:

  * `soh/include/functions.h`, `soh/soh/GbiWrap.cpp` — `gDPSetTileSizeLerp`, the
    C entry point wrapping LUS's `__gDPSetTileSizeLerp` macro.
  * `soh/src/code/z_rcp.c` — `Gfx_TexScrollEx`, `Gfx_TwoTexScrollEx` and
    `Gfx_TwoTexScrollEnvColorEx` rewritten to emit ONE lerp command per tile
    (endpoints = the tile rectangle now, and the same rectangle advanced by one
    game tick's scroll step) instead of a `gDPSetInterpolation` +
    `gDPSetTileSizeInterp` pair per interpolated frame. Allocation becomes a
    constant 7 / 12 / 13 Gfx instead of `2 + 4n` / `5 + 7n` / `6 + 7n`.
    `Gfx_TwoTexScrollEnvColorEx` is the Death Mountain Crater lava and Water
    Temple water path this whole round is about.
  * `soh/soh/OTRGlobals.h` + `.cpp` — upstream DELETES
    `Ship_GetInterpolationFrameCount` here (z_rcp.c was its only caller, verified
    by grep across `vendor/Shipwright/soh` and `app/`, and nothing needs the frame
    count once display lists stop scaling with it). WE KEEP IT. Overlay 0013
    anchors its own additions on that function's body as patch context, so
    deleting it makes 0013 undetectable on a second `apply-overlay.sh` run and
    hard-fails the build — the same trap as `RunCommands` below. It is now an
    unreferenced `extern "C"` helper: a few dead instructions, zero risk, and
    removable in a later round that also regenerates 0013.
  * `ovl_Boss_Tw`, `ovl_En_Fz`, `ovl_Effect_Ss_En_Ice` — three call sites moved
    from `Gfx_TwoTexScroll` to `Gfx_TwoTexScrollEx` with their real per-tick
    scroll steps, so those effects interpolate instead of stepping at 20 Hz.

THE ONE PLACE WE DIVERGE, AND WHY.

Upstream's `RunCommands(Gfx*, int time, int step, int denom, int count)` runs the
interpolation loop itself, so its fix is a one-liner inside that loop:
`intp->mInterpolationT = (float)time / denom;`. OUR `RunCommands` has a different
shape — it takes a PRE-BUILT `std::vector<std::unordered_map<Mtx*, MtxF>>` that
`Graph_ProcessGfxCommands` fills in a separate loop — and, more to the point,
overlay 0008's perf probe owns EVERY line of its body and its signature as patch
context (`void RunCommands(...)`, `for (const auto& m : mtx_replacements) {`,
`wnd->DrawAndRunGraphicsCommands(Commands, m);`, `intp->mInterpolationIndex++;`,
and the call site `RunCommands(commands, mtx_replacements);`). Editing any of
them makes 0008 undetectable on a second `apply-overlay.sh` run — which is not a
style objection, it is a hard build failure: the script's reverse-apply probe
fails and reports "0008 neither applied nor appliable". (Observed, not theorised;
this patch was written the other way first.)

So `RunCommands` is not touched at all. Instead `Graph_ProcessGfxCommands`:

  * builds a parallel `std::vector<float> interp_ts`, pushing
    `(float)time / next_original_frame` next to each matrix replacement — the
    SAME expression it already passes to `FrameInterpolation_Interpolate`, so
    matrices and tile sizes are interpolated to the identical instant, and the
    last frame of a tick is exactly 1.0f (upstream's `time == denom` case);
  * hands the whole list to the interpreter once per tick, next to the existing
    `wnd->SetTargetFps(fps)`;
  * does the same in the gfx-debugger branch, which collapses the frame list to a
    single entry, with a single 1.0f.

Overlay 0057's last two hunks are the LUS side of that: `mInterpolationTs`, indexed by the
`mInterpolationIndex` the loop already advances per rendered frame, with
`mInterpolationT` kept as the fallback. Net effect is identical to upstream's
one-liner; the difference is only WHERE the factor is handed over.

WHY THAT COVERS BOTH PLATFORMS. `Graph_ProcessGfxCommands` -> `RunCommands` is
the ONLY path that runs graphics commands in this port (grep for `RunCommands`
across `vendor/Shipwright/soh` and `app/` finds exactly one definition and one
call). Flat iOS drives it from the display link; the visionOS immersive path
drives the same game loop — `app/ios/SohImmersive.m` changes what
`GetInterpolationFPS()` REPORTS (overlay 0015), which feeds `target_fps` in this
very function and therefore the length of both vectors. So the LERP factor
advances once per rendered frame on iPhone and in the headset alike, and the
scroll cannot freeze on either.

